  "caption": "🦟 Dengue in children: one Reel for wards & exams | PG Notes · Infectious Diseases\n\n🧬 Basics: Flavivirus, DENV 1–4 · Aedes aegypti (day-biter) · incubation 4–10 days · secondary heterotypic infection → ADE → higher risk of severe dengue\n\n📅 Phases:\n• Febrile 2–7 d (leukopenia early)\n• Critical at defervescence, ~day 3–7: plasma leak 24–48 h\n• Recovery 48–72 h: reabsorption, bradycardia, rash, fluid-overload risk\n\n📋 WHO 2009: dengue ± warning signs · severe dengue (severe leak → shock/resp distress · severe bleeding · organ involvement, AST/ALT ≥ 1000)\n\n🚨 Warning signs: abdominal pain · persistent vomiting · ascites/effusion · mucosal bleed · lethargy/restlessness · liver > 2 cm · rising HCT with rapid platelet fall\n\n🧪 Tests: NS1/RT-PCR day 1–5 · IgM ELISA after day 5 · serial HCT (≥ 20% rise = leak) · tourniquet test ≥ 10 petechiae/sq inch\n\n💧 Warning signs: crystalloid 5–7 → 3–5 → 2–3 mL/kg/h; if HCT rises 5–10 mL/kg/h\n⚡ Compensated shock: 10–20 mL/kg over 1 h → 10 → 7 → 5 → 3 mL/kg/h; no response: HCT high → colloid 10–20 mL/kg; HCT falling → bleeding, transfuse\n🔴 Hypotensive shock: 20 mL/kg over 15 min; HCT falling → whole blood 10–20 mL/kg or PRBC 5–10 mL/kg\n\n✅ Monitor vitals, urine (~0.5 mL/kg/h), serial HCT · ❌ NSAIDs, IM injections, steroids, prophylactic platelets\n\n📚 Ref: WHO Dengue Guidelines 2009 & WHO Handbook 2012\nFollow @paediatrick_or_treats & save 📌\n\nFor medical education. Verify with current guidelines and your unit protocol.\n\n#dengue #denguefever #severedengue #paediatrics #pediatrics #pgnotes #neetpg #inicet #pediatricresident #medstudent #highyield #infectiousdiseases #picu #medicos #mbbs"
